# The Linux baseline Ephervex releases target.
#
# This is a support commitment, not a description of whichever machine happened
# to run the build. `build-appimage.sh` reads it and refuses to package binaries
# that require anything newer, so a newer build host produces a hard failure
# instead of quietly raising the floor for everyone.
#
# Changing these values changes what Ephervex claims to run on. See
# docs/adr/0023-appimage-is-the-linux-release-artifact.md and docs/release.md.

# Minimum glibc. 2.35 is Ubuntu 22.04 LTS and Linux Mint 21; Debian 12 ships
# 2.36 and every later release is higher still.
EPHERVEX_BASELINE_GLIBC=2.35

# The distribution image whose toolchain reaches that floor. The release build
# runs inside this; the packaging script never pulls or manages it.
EPHERVEX_BASELINE_IMAGE=ubuntu:22.04

# Every architecture Ephervex releases for. packaging/linux-architectures is
# where each one's Debian, RPM, AppImage, and dynamic-linker spelling lives.
EPHERVEX_BASELINE_ARCHITECTURES="x86_64 aarch64"

# The architecture this build targets, which defaults to the host.
#
# Selectable rather than fixed, but never cross-compiled: the glibc floor above,
# the shared-library allowlist, and the smoke tests all read and run the *built
# binary*, and a build for an architecture the host cannot execute leaves every
# one of them unverified. The packaging scripts enforce that, so naming an
# architecture this machine is not is a hard failure rather than a cross build.
EPHERVEX_BASELINE_ARCH=${EPHERVEX_BASELINE_ARCH:-$(uname -m)}
